	<!-- ─── Comprehensive Educational Section: Sensor Fusion ─── -->
	<section id="engineering" class="border-t border-hairline bg-canvas scroll-mt-16">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 md:py-28">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
				<p class="eyebrow">sensor physics & computer science</p>
				<h2 class="mt-3 text-display-lg text-ink">
					The Engineering Behind Browser-Based Sensor Fusion
				</h2>
				<p class="mt-6 text-body-lg text-body leading-relaxed">
					Most people assume a smartphone compass is just a simple digital needle reading a magnet. In reality, producing a stable, jitter-free 360-degree heading on a handheld glass screen is one of the most sophisticated feats of modern electrical and aerospace engineering.
				</p>
				<p class="mt-4 text-body leading-7">
					When you open this website on a mobile device, our software establishes a direct pipeline to the W3C <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-canvas-soft px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded border border-hairline">DeviceOrientationEvent</code> and <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-canvas-soft px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded border border-hairline">DeviceMotionEvent</code> APIs. Through these interfaces, the browser queries three distinct micro-electro-mechanical systems (MEMS) fabricated onto silicon microchips:
				</p>

				<div class="mt-8 space-y-6">
					<div class="card p-6">
						<h3 class="text-display-sm text-ink">1. The Triaxial Magnetometer (Flux Sensor)</h3>
						<p class="mt-2 text-sm text-body leading-6">
							Operating on either the Hall effect or anisotropic magnetoresistance (AMR), the magnetometer measures ambient magnetic flux density along three orthogonal axes (X, Y, and Z) in microteslas (µT). Earth's magnetic field is remarkably faint—averaging between 30 and 60 µT—making the sensor extraordinarily susceptible to localized electromagnetic noise.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card p-6">
						<h3 class="text-display-sm text-ink">2. The Triaxial Capacitive Accelerometer (Gravity Reference)</h3>
						<p class="mt-2 text-sm text-body leading-6">
							Microscopic polysilicon proof masses suspended on silicon springs deflect under the pull of Earth's gravitational acceleration (1g ≈ 9.80665 m/s²). By monitoring differential capacitance changes across fixed plates, the accelerometer determines which direction is "down," providing pitch (β) and roll (γ) angles.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card p-6">
						<h3 class="text-display-sm text-ink">3. The Triaxial Vibratory Gyroscope (Rotational Velocity)</h3>
						<p class="mt-2 text-sm text-body leading-6">
							Vibrating silicon rings experience Coriolis forces when rotated, generating voltage signals proportional to angular rate of change (rad/s). While gyroscopes are immune to magnetic distortion, they suffer from mechanical integration drift over time.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<h3 class="mt-12 text-display-sm text-ink">The Mathematical Solution: Real-Time Tilt Compensation</h3>
				<p class="mt-3 text-body leading-7">
					A traditional magnetic needle only works when held perfectly flat. If tilted, gravity pulls the needle downward, causing severe "dip error." A digital compass solves this mathematically through <strong>Euler-angle rotational transformation</strong>.
				</p>
				<p class="mt-4 text-body leading-7">
					Using the pitch (θ) and roll (φ) angles measured by the accelerometer, the raw three-dimensional magnetometer readings (M<sub>x</sub>, M<sub>y</sub>, M<sub>z</sub>) are mathematically de-rotated into the horizontal plane:
				</p>

				<div class="my-6 rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft p-5 font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm text-ink overflow-x-auto">
					<p class="text-mute">// Horizontal Field Vector Projection Matrix</p>
					<p class="mt-1">X_horizontal = M_x · cos(θ) + M_z · sin(θ)</p>
					<p class="mt-1">Y_horizontal = M_x · sin(φ) · sin(θ) + M_y · cos(φ) - M_z · sin(φ) · cos(θ)</p>
					<p class="mt-3 text-mute">// Calibrated Heading in Degrees</p>
					<p class="mt-1">Magnetic_Heading = atan2(-Y_horizontal, X_horizontal) · (180 / π)</p>
				</div>

				<p class="text-body leading-7">
					Finally, our software applies a continuous <strong>spherical linear interpolation (SLERP) and low-pass filter</strong>. This damps transient hand tremor and mechanical vibration while ensuring that when you swivel your body to look in a new direction, the needle sweeps with fluid 60-frame-per-second precision.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- ─── Deep Dive: True North, Magnetic North & Declination ─── -->
	<section id="true-north" class="border-t border-hairline bg-canvas scroll-mt-16">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 md:py-28">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
				<p class="eyebrow">cartography & geodesy</p>
				<h2 class="mt-3 text-display-lg text-ink">
					True North vs. Magnetic North: The Geometry of Map Navigation
				</h2>
				<p class="mt-6 text-body-lg text-body leading-relaxed">
					One of the most dangerous misconceptions in outdoor navigation is assuming that a compass points to the top of the map. It does not. Failure to account for the difference between the three distinct "norths" is the leading cause of disorientation among hikers, mariners, and pilots.
				</p>

				<div class="mt-8 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
					<div class="card-soft p-5">
						<p class="eyebrow mb-1">geographic</p>
						<h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">True North (TN)</h3>
						<p class="mt-2 text-xs text-body leading-5">The fixed northern axis around which planet Earth rotates. All longitudinal meridians converge here. Used by maps, GPS satellites, and civil surveying.</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card-soft p-5">
						<p class="eyebrow mb-1">geomagnetic</p>
						<h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">Magnetic North (MN)</h3>
						<p class="mt-2 text-xs text-body leading-5">The shifting point in the Arctic Ocean where Earth's geomagnetic lines enter the core vertically. Moves 40–55 km each year due to molten geodynamo churn.</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card-soft p-5">
						<p class="eyebrow mb-1">projected</p>
						<h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">Grid North (GN)</h3>
						<p class="mt-2 text-xs text-body leading-5">The vertical grid lines drawn on flat projected paper maps (such as UTM or Ordnance Survey). Differs slightly from true north due to spherical distortion.</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<h3 class="mt-12 text-display-sm text-ink">The Mathematical Rule for Declination Arithmetic</h3>
				<p class="mt-3 text-body leading-7">
					The angular difference between True North and Magnetic North at your exact location is called <strong>Magnetic Declination</strong> (or <strong>Magnetic Variation</strong> in maritime nautical charts). The sign convention is governed by a fundamental navigational law:
				</p>

				<div class="my-6 rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft p-5 text-center font-mono text-sm text-ink">
					<p class="text-display-sm font-semibold text-link">True Bearing = Magnetic Bearing + Declination</p>
					<p class="text-xs text-mute mt-2">(where East Declination is positive [+] and West Declination is negative [−])</p>
				</div>

				<p class="text-body leading-7">
					Generations of naval navigators have remembered this rule using the classic mnemonic: <em>"East is least, West is best"</em> (referring to whether you subtract or add when converting from True to Magnetic).
				</p>
				<ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-sm text-body list-disc pl-5">
					<li><strong>In New York City (Declination ≈ −13° West):</strong> If your magnetic compass reads 90° (due East), your actual true heading is 90° + (−13°) = <strong>77°</strong>. You are heading 13 degrees north of true east!</li>
					<li><strong>In Los Angeles (Declination ≈ +12° East):</strong> If your magnetic compass reads 90°, your actual true heading is 90° + (+12°) = <strong>102°</strong>.</li>
				</ul>

				<p class="mt-6 text-body leading-7">
					When you tap <strong>"True North"</strong> on our online compass, our algorithm automatically applies your local NOAA / WMM declination vector, rendering arithmetic obsolete and aligning your on-screen dial directly with topographic map lines.
				</p>
			</div>

			<!-- Representative World Declination Table -->
			<div class="mx-auto mt-12 max-w-4xl overflow-x-auto">
				<table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm">
					<caption class="sr-only">Worldwide Magnetic Declination Benchmarks</caption>
					<thead>
						<tr class="border-b border-hairline-strong text-left">
							<th scope="col" class="py-3.5 pr-4 font-semibold text-ink">Global Location</th>
							<th scope="col" class="py-3.5 pr-4 font-semibold text-ink">Geomagnetic Declination</th>
							<th scope="col" class="py-3.5 font-semibold text-ink">Navigational Consequence (1 km Travel)</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody class="divide-y divide-hairline">
						{declinationExamples.map((d) => (
							<tr class="hover:bg-canvas-soft/40 transition-colors">
								<td class="py-3.5 pr-4 font-medium text-ink">{d.place}</td>
								<td class="py-3.5 pr-4 font-mono text-link font-semibold">{d.value}</td>
								<td class="py-3.5 text-body text-xs leading-5">{d.impact}</td>
							</tr>
						))}
					</tbody>
				</table>
				<p class="mt-3 text-xs text-mute">
					Values reflect the current World Magnetic Model epoch. Because Earth's geomagnetic core shifts continuously, always enable GPS location in the compass tool for real-time sub-kilometer accuracy.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

